      <acknowledgement xml:id="acknowledgements">
        <p>In addition to my colleagues, I am grateful for the many students who have helped me grow as a teacher. Thank you for your willingness to engage in this very human art of learning and for sharing your experiences, frustrations, and successes with me.</p>
      </acknowledgement>

      <preface xml:id="welcome">
        <title>Welcome</title>
        <p><em>All students, not just those headed for science and engineering, should develop a mathematical viewpoint, including critical thinking, problem-solving strategies, and estimation, in addition to computational skills.</em></p>
        <p>— Katherine Yoshiwara, <url href="https://runestone.academy/ns/books/published/mfg/preface.html" visual="Modeling, Functions, and Graphs, Preface"/>.</p>
        <p>Mathematics is for everyone. Calculating correctly matters, but so do understanding ideas, choosing methods, estimating, making connections, testing claims and explaining why an answer makes sense.</p>
        <p><em>Irish School Maths</em> is an evolving collection of explanations, examples, investigations, exercises and classroom resources. The aim is not simply to deliver content, but to make mathematics something students can explore, understand and use.</p>
        <p>The <url href="https://ronandownes.github.io/maths/" visual="Maths Apps"/>, <url href="https://ronandownes.github.io/calculator/" visual="calculators"/> and <url href="https://ronandownes.github.io/pastpapers/" visual="past papers"/> are companions to the book. Further <url href="https://ronandownes.github.io/calculator/resources/" visual="free textbooks and mathematics resources"/> are also collected separately.</p>
      </preface>

      <preface xml:id="about-the-author">
        <title>About the Author</title>
        <p>Ronan Downes is a Mathematics teacher based in Limerick, Ireland. He studied Physics at NUI Galway and later graduated with an honours degree in Physical Optoelectronics from the University of Essex. After completing a Higher Diploma in Education, he taught Physics, Mathematics and Science in secondary schools for four years.</p>
        <p>He then moved into telecommunications, working as a systems engineer and access engineer with Esat Digifone. He later became self-employed in multimedia, including photography, video production and web development.</p>
        <p>A lifelong interest in learning brought him back to university, where he completed a <url href="https://www.ul.ie/study/postgraduate/mathematical-modelling-msc" visual="Master's degree in Mathematical Modelling"/> at the University of Limerick. He subsequently returned to classroom teaching and later completed a <url href="https://www.cct.ie/course/masters-data-analytics-postgraduate-msc/" visual="Master's degree in Data Analytics"/> at CCT College Dublin. His experience in modelling, programming and computational thinking informs the explanations and interactive resources in this book.</p>
        <p>He has also worked for many years as an examiner with the State Examinations Commission. That experience has reinforced the importance of teaching students to communicate their mathematical thinking.</p>
        <p>His aim is to reduce unnecessary cognitive load without removing the ideas, reasoning and choices that give mathematics its meaning. His guiding principle is inspired by a saying commonly attributed to Albert Einstein: <em>Making maths as simple as possible, but not too simple.</em></p>
      </preface>

      <preface xml:id="project-preface">
        <title>Preface</title>
        <p>Welcome to <em>Irish School Maths</em>, an open, evolving mathematics resource for the Irish post-primary education system, freely available to anyone interested in learning mathematics.</p>
        <p>As the subtitle, <em>Making Maths as Simple as Possible</em>, suggests, the emphasis is on clear explanations, explicit modelling and an organised, accessible interface. Graphs, tables, diagrams and algebraic expressions offer complementary ways of understanding mathematical ideas. The intention is to introduce these representations early, return to them throughout the book and connect procedures with concepts and practical applications.</p>
        <p>Our students progress through Junior Cycle, with the option of Transition Year (TY), and onwards to the Leaving Certificate. Competition for college places can make the Leaving Certificate particularly stressful, with pressures sometimes extending back into TY.</p>
        <p>Transition Year offers a wonderful opportunity for teachers and students to be more creative, to investigate, collaborate and engage in project-based learning. Yet it can become dominated by revisiting Junior Cycle procedures or teaching Leaving Certificate material in advance, from complex numbers and general-triangle trigonometry to Pascal's triangle and higher-order polynomials. These topics have their place, but the TY section of this book will concentrate on exploration, investigation, real-world applications and the development of independent learners.</p>
        <p><em>The procedural fluency trap</em></p>
        <p>One of my central convictions is that we cannot develop the habit of mathematical problem-solving by teaching procedures first and introducing problem-solving afterwards.</p>
        <p>I call this <em>the procedural fluency trap</em>: the assumption that students must master procedural fluency before developing other areas of mathematical proficiency, such as conceptual understanding, strategic competence and adaptive reasoning.</p>
        <p>Procedural fluency is essential, but it must develop alongside these other proficiencies. If we teach only one procedure for applying a mathematical principle, students have little opportunity to compare methods, exercise judgement or make meaningful strategic choices.</p>
        <p><em>Problem-solving is not simply the final application of mathematical learning. It is one of the ways mathematical learning takes place.</em></p>
        <p>That philosophy runs throughout this book. I want students to explore mathematical ideas, recognise connections, discover underlying principles and appreciate the beauty and sometimes surprising simplicity of mathematics.</p>
        <p>The explanations, worked examples, exercises, quizzes and interactive applications are designed to encourage understanding, independence and curiosity. Students should be able to practise procedures, investigate alternatives, check their reasoning and return to earlier material whenever necessary.</p>
        <p>The book is developed using <em>PreTeXt</em>, an open-source publishing system for mathematical textbooks. It allows mathematics to be presented clearly and accessibly, with interactive resources, structured navigation and printable versions.</p>
        <p>This is a developing project, not a finished textbook. Its content, organisation and scope will continue to evolve.</p>
        <p>Above all, I hope to build a community of students, teachers, parents, special needs assistants and others who share an interest in better mathematics education. Your ideas, experiences and contributions are very welcome.</p>
        <p>I hope this book helps make mathematics clearer and encourages students to become confident, curious and independent mathematical thinkers.</p>
        <p><em>Ronan Downes</em></p>
      </preface>

      <preface xml:id="for-students">
        <title>For Students</title>
        <p>Start with the mathematics you are studying. You do not need to read the whole book from the beginning, or work through all the earlier material before you reach your own course. If you need to revisit a basic idea, follow the contents or a link back to the relevant explanation, then return to where you left off.</p>
        <p>Try to do more than follow a worked solution. Make a prediction, attempt a question, test an idea, and explain your reasoning. Use the interactive apps, diagrams and exercises to investigate what changes and what stays the same.</p>
        <p>Learning outcomes help you see what you are aiming to understand. Worked examples show a method; exercises give you the opportunity to make that method your own. When answers are available, use them to check your reasoning rather than to replace it.</p>
        <p>This is a developing book. Some topics are more complete than others, and the organisation may change as the resource grows.</p>
      </preface>

      <preface xml:id="for-teachers-course-structure">
        <title>For Teachers and Course Structure</title>
        <p><em>Why another mathematics textbook?</em></p>
        <p>There are excellent textbooks already, along with interactive tools such as <url href="https://www.geogebra.org/" visual="GeoGebra"/> and rich mathematical tasks at <url href="https://nrich.maths.org/" visual="NRICH"/>. Why write, host and share another book for free?</p>
        <p>When I returned to teaching, I was struck by how often a single level of explanation or exercise left some students needing more support and others ready for greater challenge. A digital textbook offers an opportunity to respond more flexibly. Links can take a student straight to a prerequisite explanation, an alternative method or a more demanding task, and then back to the topic in hand. These connections can be made in context, without searching through chapters or changing books. Most teachers have answered <q>What page are we on?</q> more times than they care to remember; here, a link can take everyone to precisely the right place.</p>
        <p>The same applies to schoolwork and homework. A teacher can share a link to a particular section, worked example or set of exercises through Teams or another learning platform, rather than asking students to find a page. This does not replace a well-planned lesson, but it can make resources easier to find, revisit and use independently.</p>
        <p><em>Features of the text</em></p>
        <ul>
          <li><p><em>Clear explanations and worked examples:</em> explicit modelling alongside opportunities to ask why a method works, not merely how to follow it.</p></li>
          <li><p><em>Interactivity and visualisation:</em> graphs, diagrams, GeoGebra resources and interactive applications to help students explore what changes, what stays the same and how different representations connect.</p></li>
          <li><p><em>Differentiation in both directions:</em> accessible routes back to foundational ideas and routes forward to extension, investigation and more challenging problems.</p></li>
          <li><p><em>Navigation and sharing:</em> a searchable, linked structure in which individual pages and exercises can be reached and assigned directly, with printable material where useful.</p></li>
        </ul>
        <p>This is an open, developing project, and I would like to work with other teachers. If you start using the book, please tell me what works, what needs improving and what you would like to see added. Explanations, classroom investigations, worked examples, worksheets, assessments, interactive activities and other good teaching materials are all welcome for discussion. With agreement and appropriate credit, worthwhile contributions can become part of the project. You can <url href="https://github.com/ronandownes/book/issues" visual="start a conversation on the project's GitHub page"/>.</p>
        <p>For now, I am keeping the structure deliberately simple. Material is grouped chiefly by course and mathematical strand, rather than arranged as a prescribed chronological teaching sequence. I recognise that there are pedagogical arguments for other arrangements, and that students do not always learn topics in a neat strand-by-strand order. Nevertheless, I would rather use the contents menu to navigate between topics than make the menu itself a record of one particular teaching sequence. Teachers can teach in the order that suits their students, making connections across strands as they go.</p>
        <p>The structure is not fixed. It is open to discussion and can change as the project develops. My preference is for useful, descriptive titles. Labels such as <q>Algebra 1</q>, <q>Algebra 2</q> and <q>Algebra 3</q> tell a reader very little about what is inside. Where broad distinctions are helpful, established descriptions such as <em>elementary algebra</em>, <em>intermediate algebra</em> and <em>advanced algebra</em> communicate more; within those areas, titles such as <em>Expanding Brackets</em>, <em>Factorisation</em>, <em>Equations</em> and <em>Functions</em> are more informative still. The same principle applies to geometry and other areas of mathematics.</p>
        <p>Keeping the material together also allows a student working at a higher level to go straight to the relevant topic without being led through elementary work, while retaining an easy route back to earlier Junior Cycle ideas when they are needed. The book can be divided into separate course editions later if that becomes useful, without giving up those connections.</p>
        <p>These pages are resources for teaching and learning, not a substitute for professional judgement about sequencing, differentiation or the needs of an individual class. I welcome suggestions, corrections and contributions.</p>
      </preface>
    </frontmatter>

