<part xml:id="part-jc-statistics">
      <title>Statistics and Probability</title>
      <xi:include href="./jc-statistics-outcomes.ptx" />
<chapter xml:id="chapter-probability"><title>Events and Counting</title><objectives><title>Learning Intentions</title><introduction><p>After completing this chapter, you should be able to:</p></introduction><ol><li><p>Use the fundamental counting principle.</p></li><li><p>Explain why the counting rule applies.</p></li></ol></objectives><introduction><p>Probability begins with a precise account of possible outcomes.</p></introduction>
              <section xml:id="probability-fcp"><title>The Fundamental Counting Principle</title>
                <p>When the first choice has <m>m</m> possibilities and the second has <m>n</m> possibilities, every combination of choices gives <m>mn</m> possibilities.</p>
                <exercise xml:id="exercise-fcp">
                  <statement><p>How many two-character codes can be formed from three choices for the first character and four for the second, if every pair is allowed?</p></statement>
                  <answer><p><m>3\cdot4=12</m>.</p></answer>
                </exercise>
              </section>
            </chapter>
    </part>

